Why: the encoder sizes the coded picture from the source height AFTER the source
alignment has been applied to it, so the source buffer's padding rows end up
inside the stream. The two sides have different rules: W5_ENC_RAW_STEP_HEIGHT is
16, W5_ENC_CODEC_STEP_HEIGHT is 8. A caller asking to encode 1920x1080 gets a
source geometry of 1088 rows, which is correct and is what the buffer must hold,
and then a 1088-row coded picture, which is not: 1080 is already a multiple of
the coded step, so nothing required the picture to grow.

The visible result is eight rows of whatever the frame writer left in the tail of
its allocation, encoded as image and shown by every decoder. On this SoC the CVISP
capture node writes exactly 1080 rows into a 1088-row buffer, so those rows carry
unrelated resident data and read as a shifted strip along the bottom edge.

Take the caller's requested picture size for the coded side and let the codec's own
frmsize constraints round it, instead of inheriting a rounding that belongs to the
source buffer. The source geometry is untouched, so the frame buffer the hardware
reads is unchanged.

